WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.250
Comic book fans the world over are starting
to realize that Black Panther just might've

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.020
changed superhero movies forever.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.500
From its jaw-dropping aesthetic choices to
its cast of butt-kicking women, Ryan Coogler's

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.290
Black Panther oozes style — and substance
— and the cinematic landscape is better

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:14.780
for it.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.080
Think back to Phase One of the MCU.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.259
Remember the great jokes, the impressive costume
design, the heartfelt expressions of heroism.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.660
Now try to remember every female character
from those movies as you can.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.860
Spoiler alert: They're mostly the girlfriends.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.610
You've got Pepper Potts, Peggy Carter, and
Jane Foster... plus some side characters like

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.640
the irrepressible Darcy, Sif, Frigga, and,
ultimately, the introduction of Black Widow.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.380
It's not the most dire situation in Hollywood,
but generally speaking, if you're not there

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.690
to smooch, you're either set dressing or the
one very special lady Avenger who still spends

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.020
a chunk of every movie trying to seduce her
prey.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.500
Even Phase Two's introduction of Gamora in
Guardians of the Galaxy fell into the same

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.860
pattern, as she's relentlessly pursued by
Peter Quill throughout the film.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:57.940
"NO!"

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.720
"What the hell?!"

00:00:58.720 --> 00:00:59.720
"I know who you are, Peter Quill!

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.880
And I am not some starry-eyed waif here to
succumb to your… your…

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.140
PELVIC SORCERY!"

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.660
Then came Black Panther in Phase Three and
its cast full of women.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.760
There's Nakia, Shuri, Okoye and the Dora Milaje,
Queen Ramonda, and women filling out the royal

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:15.260
cabinet.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.940
Women were everywhere in Black Panther, doing
everything.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.840
Both within the MCU and outside of it, this
was pretty revolutionary.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.469
While Captain Marvel's starring role in 2019
was in development for years, Black Panther's

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:30.860
amazing cast of women may have gotten audiences
ready to receive the film a year earlier.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.400
Blockbuster movies don't typically bet on
the unfamiliar — which makes Black Panther

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.820
feel like kind of a miracle.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.200
It has a majority black cast, takes place
in a fictional African nation inspired by

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.039
real African cultures, and features actors
speaking Xhosa, an official language of Zimbabwe

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.060
and South Africa.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.680
It is deeply rooted in specific places, histories,
and aesthetics that most of the worldwide

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.200
audience knows little about.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.340
And it made over $1 billion worldwide.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.219
Black Panther didn't just prove that people
all over the world can embrace lives outside

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:02.751
their own spheres of experience — it proved
that they can fall in love with them, cheer

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.340
them on, and come back for more.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.160
Superhero movies tend to look the same.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.620
Save for a signature color or a formalwear
scene, it's all based in ultramodern tech

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:12.540
and military drab.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.080
This can be an interesting look, of course,
but it does tend to blend together into a

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.260
canvas of olive green, stainless steel, and
dark suits.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.780
Thor shook this up admirably, and, with the
release of the positively psychedelic Thor:

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.680
Ragnarok, cemented its branch of the MCU as
a risk-taker.

00:02:26.909 --> 00:02:31.220
For a while, you had three choices: you could
be surrounded by SHIELD black or army base

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:32.920
grey, or you could be in Asgard.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:36.300
The thing about all of these aesthetics, even
Thor's, is that they're familiar.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:41.060
We know how action movies look, how high fantasy
looks, how military movies look.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.780
The MCU did them all well, but they'd been
done before.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.860
Then in strides Black Panther, with its wealth
of color, texture, and fashion both ancient

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:49.840
and contemporary.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.599
Audiences saw the deep green of the River
Tribe, the bright coral of the Dora Milaje,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.580
and Shuri's up-to-the-minute bomber jackets.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.980
Costume designer Ruth E. Carter, who won an
Oscar for her work on Black Panther, managed

00:03:00.989 --> 00:03:06.190
to bring color, history, and an entirely unique
Afrofuturistic aesthetic the majority of the

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.620
audience was unfamiliar with.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.240
It's not just cohesive, it's not just new
to the genre, it's not just rooted in an absolute

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.200
avalanche of research — it's all of those
things and more, packed into one movie.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.560
Marvel's home base has always been New York
City.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.200
This has done a lot to bring a unique sense
of place to the stories that take place within

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.500
it: Daredevil, She-Hulk, and Spider-Man all
live in locations you can visit, that their

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:32.250
creators grew up in, that mirror the rapid
changes of 20th and 21st-century life with

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.580
a specificity that's hard to replicate in
Metropolis or Gotham.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.780
Peter Parker is a boy from Queens, just as
Daredevil is a child of Hell's Kitchen.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:44.300
The city, to use a well-worn but very true
cliche, is as much a Marvel character as Captain

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:44.800
America.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.640
But that can start to feel a bit confining,
especially when the same city is splashed

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.760
across the screen over and over again.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.820
Marvel branched out, for sure — Guardians
of the Galaxy went far afield indeed — but

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:58.340
there wasn't anywhere with the same strength
of location until Wakanda came along.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:03.939
Rooted in painstaking research of actual African
cities and cultures, Wakanda looks, sounds,

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.040
and feels like a real place with real history.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.480
"Sister Nakia.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:08.980
My Prince.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:11.580
We are home."

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.640
Audiences agreed, cheering when the heroes
returned to Wakanda in Infinity War.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.800
Black Panther didn't just show that audiences
can invest in MCU locations beyond NYC and

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.580
outer space — they can feel right at home
there too.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:25.780
"Wakanda forever!"

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.640
T'challa isn't the first royal in the MCU.
Thor, of course, begins his journey as heir

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.000
to the throne of Asgard, on a path to discovering
what makes someone worthy of that authority.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.060
Loki has been twisted by his desire for kingly
power into a perennial villain.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.140
Both men — as one might expect — find
themselves often engaging in battle.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.060
This isn't a flaw — these are, after all,
action movies.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.280
But T'Challa's kingship is defined as much
by his role in international diplomacy as

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.400
his ability to send forces into battle.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.270
Much of Black Panther is concerned with how
best to share Wakanda's resources with the

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:02.830
rest of the world, how to aid in global conflicts
responsibly, and, tellingly, how violence

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.760
has typically led to tragic outcomes in Wakandan
history.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.920
We don't see T'Challa commanding conquering
armies — rather, we see him consulting his

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.610
advisers, speaking at the United Nations,
and engaging in ceremonial combat as a means

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:15.760
of transferring power.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.180
"Wakanda will no longer watch from the shadows.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:20.900
We cannot.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.160
We must not."

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.319
T'Challa is a diplomat first and a warrior
second, a man who leads his troops into battle

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:28.840
reluctantly.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.620
Superheroes, post-Black Panther, aren't confined
to the Pentagon, military bases, or other

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:38.080
martial visions of world power — they debate
others, open community centers, and strive

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.560
for global cooperation.

